Execute code branches based on conditions.

<pre><code>x = 10
if x > 0:
    print("Positive")
elif x == 0:
    print("Zero")
else:
    print("Negative")</code></pre>

A function can call itself (recursion). Always include a base case to end recursion. Recursive solutions are common for tasks like factorial or tree traversal, but can hit recursion limits.

<pre><code>def factorial(n):
    if n == 0:
        return 1
    else:
        return n * factorial(n-1)

print(factorial(5))  # 120</code></pre>

Sets are <b>unordered</b> collections of unique items (mutable). Useful for membership tests and deduplication.

<pre><code>unique = {1, 2, 3, 2, 1}
print(unique)  # {1,2,3}
unique.add(4)
unique.remove(2)
</code></pre>

Special methods (dunder methods) implement built-in behaviors. E.g., `__str__`, `__repr__` control string representation; `__add__`, `__eq__` overload operators.

<pre><code>class Vector:
    def __init__(self, x, y): self.x, self.y = x, y
    def __add__(self, other):
        return Vector(self.x+other.x, self.y+other.y)
    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})"

v1 = Vector(1,2)
v2 = Vector(3,4)
print(v1 + v2)  # Vector(4, 6)</code></pre>

Example of Fibonacci sequence:

<pre><code># Iterative version
def fib_iter(n):
    a, b = 0, 1
    for _ in range(n):
        a, b = b, a + b
    return a

# Recursive version
def fib_rec(n):
    if n < 2:
        return n
    return fib_rec(n-1) + fib_rec(n-2)
</code></pre>
